---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GearRatio from '@/components/tools/calc2/GearRatio.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="gear-ratio-calculator">
  <GearRatio client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the gear ratio calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Meshing gears move the same number of teeth past the contact point. A gear with more teeth therefore
      turns more slowly. The gear ratio calculator divides the teeth of the driven gear by the teeth of
      the driver gear and applies the ratio to speed and torque.
    </p>
    <pre><code>ratio          = driven teeth ÷ driver teeth
output speed   = input speed ÷ ratio
output torque  = input torque × ratio
power          = torque × 2π × rpm ÷ 60     (the same in and out)</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 20 tooth driver at 1,200 rpm meshes with a 60 tooth gear. The ratio is 60 ÷ 20 = 3, written 3 : 1.
      The output turns at 1,200 ÷ 3 = 400 rpm in the opposite direction, and 10 N·m of input torque
      becomes 30 N·m.
    </p>

    <h2>Idler gears and gear trains</h2>
    <p>
      An idler between the two gears turns the output in the same direction as the input. Its tooth count
      has no effect on the ratio. In a compound train, with two gears fixed on one shaft, multiply the
      ratios of the stages: 3 : 1 followed by 4 : 1 gives 12 : 1.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The torque assumes no friction. A pair of spur gears typically loses 1 to 3% of the power; a worm
      gear can lose 30% or more. The drawing is to scale: each gear's pitch circle is proportional to its
      teeth, as it is for gears of one module.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
